WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
I built CaptureProof for merchants who need to know whether a failed checkout request left a successful payment behind.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Real PayPal sandbox requests, test funds only. Buyer approval was completed before recording.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:21.000
The capture succeeds. Its response is deliberately withheld from checkout. This injected transport fault is labelled.

4
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Reconciliation reads the order again. Gemini explains the records and links its findings to the evidence.

5
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:39.000
A matching completed capture means the payment must not be repeated. Inspect the linked response and request trace.

6
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.000
One capture and one merchant receipt. Another capture is blocked by transaction-state checks.

7
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Replay of a recorded sandbox rejection: ORDER_NOT_APPROVED. A rejected request before approval, not a declined card.

8
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.000
The same order has since been approved in sandbox. Approval alone has not captured it. Recovery needs a separate merchant action.

9
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:16.080
Explicit recovery checks current approval, amount and absence of captures, then makes one capture request. One receipt is recorded.

10
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:27.080
This captured transaction was delivered twice in a local replay. It is not a webhook received from PayPal. The duplicate was ignored.

11
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:44.440
One capture and one receipt per checkout. The console runs locally with Node and SQLite, and exports redacted evidence.
